{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Moses reported the words of the people to HaShem.</b> This verse is entirely superfluous, for it has already been stated, ״וַיָּשֶׁב מֹשֶׁה אֶת דִּבְרֵי הָעָם אֶל ה׳״ (Exodus 19:8). And Rashi explained that he told the Holy One, blessed be He, how Israel said, 'Our desire is to see our King,' and this matter is not explicit in Scripture. And perhaps it is difficult for Rashi that the words 'אֶל ה׳' appear superfluous, for he should have said 'וַיַּגֵּד מֹשֶׁה אֶת דִּבְרֵי הָעָם אֵלָיו' — since it is with HaShem that he is speaking. But because this is a response to what the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him — ״בַּעֲבוּר יִשְׁמַע הָעָם בְּדַבְּרִי עִמָּךְ וְגַם בְּךָ יַאֲמִינוּ לְעוֹלָם״ (Exodus 19:9) — and they said, 'We will hear to HaShem,' and not to Moses, therefore it says 'וַיַּגֵּד מֹשֶׁה אֶת דִּבְרֵי הָעָם,' and what are their words? On this it says 'אֶל ה׳,' for they said, 'To HaShem we will hear,' and not to you. Yet Scripture still does not explain where Moses heard from them that they said this, and furthermore it is difficult why above it says 'וַיָּשֶׁב מֹשֶׁה' (Exodus 19:8) and here it says 'וַיַּגֵּד מֹשֶׁה.'" "But this is its explanation. Because it says ״וַיָּבֹא מֹשֶׁה וַיִּקְרָא לְזִקְנֵי הָעָם״ etc. (Exodus 19:7), ״וַיַּעֲנוּ כָל הָעָם כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ נַעֲשֶׂה״ (Exodus 19:8) — but surely the elders had not yet said anything to them, so how did they answer immediately? Rather, certainly, as soon as they saw that Moses spoke his words to the elders, they immediately understood that all matters would be by way of intermediation: the Holy One, blessed be He, would speak to Moses, and Moses to the elders, and the elders to Israel. Immediately Israel jumped to answer Moses, and thereby indicated that they had no desire for the intermediation of the elders. And in saying ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ נַעֲשֶׂה״ they indicated that they did not desire the intermediation of Moses either, and said: we will do all that HaShem will speak to us, not what you will speak to us. And their reasoning was with them, for their desire was to see their King — for they could have simply said 'כֵּן נַעֲשֶׂה,' but instead they said 'כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ נַעֲשֶׂה,' to exclude the words of Moses. They also hinted, in saying ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ נַעֲשֶׂה״, and the Holy One, blessed be He, had spoken ״וְעַתָּה אִם שָׁמוֹעַ תִּשְׁמְעוּ בְּקֹלִי״ (Exodus 19:5) — hear from this that HaShem wanted them to hear the voice of HaShem speaking with them directly, not through intermediation. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him ״אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר תְּדַבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 19:6) — you yourself, not through any intermediation. And Moses went and told the elders first, and it displeased Israel greatly that HaShem would speak with them through two intermediaries. Therefore they said ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ נַעֲשֶׂה״ — to hear literally in the voice of HaShem. Consequently, the first time Moses did not report back to HaShem anything except the matter on which the essence of the mission depended, namely what they said — 'we will do all the words of HaShem' — and the language of ״וַיָּשֶׁב״ (Exodus 19:8) is appropriate here. And Moses still did not say anything to HaShem of what he understood from them — that their desire was to see their King — for Moses thought: surely Elohim understands their way and their thought, and will do according to their desire. But when the Holy One, blessed be He, returned and said to him ״הִנֵּה אָנֹכִי בָּא אֵלֶיךָ בְּעַב הֶעָנָן״ etc. (Exodus 19:9), ״וְגַם בְּךָ יַאֲמִינוּ לְעוֹלָם״ (ibid.), then he understood that the Holy One, blessed be He, still wished to speak with them through intermediation. Then ״וַיַּגֵּד מֹשֶׁה אֶת דִּבְרֵי הָעָם אֶל ה׳״ (Exodus 19:9) — he told Him what they had said, ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ נַעֲשֶׂה״, and the language of הַגָּדָה is appropriate here and not the language of תְּשׁוּבָה, for this matter is not a response regarding the essence of the mission, but merely a recounting of all that happened with them. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: if so, then, do this: ״לֵךְ אֶל הָעָם וְקִדַּשְׁתָּם הַיּוֹם וּמָחָר״ (Exodus 19:10) — for to see the face of their King they need to be holy, for ״קָדוֹשׁ אָנִי״ (Leviticus 19:2), ״וּבַיּוֹם הַשְּׁלִישִׁי יֵרֵד ה׳ לְעֵינֵי כָל הָעָם״ (Exodus 19:11), and then they will see the face of their King. And in truth He did begin to speak with them Himself, for they heard the first two commandments from the mouth of the Gevurah, until they were afraid of the fire and the sounds. Then they retracted from their request and said ״דַּבֵּר אַתָּה עִמָּנוּ וְנִשְׁמָעָה״ (Exodus 20:16)." "And as for what is written, <b>״Behold, I am coming to you in the thickness of the cloud.״</b> Because a person can never use two senses equally at once — to see and to hear simultaneously — therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said: if I reveal myself to them openly, they will set their heart and mind upon the sight, to see their King, and will not set their heart upon the hearing. Therefore I will come in the thickness of the cloud, so that they will not be able to see, in order to strengthen the sense of hearing. This is what it says: ״בַּעֲבוּר יִשְׁמַע הָעָם״ — 'in order that the people may hear.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:39.194371659Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}